TYPE 2 — SHORTEST PATH
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Find minimum steps/cost between two nodes

Rule:
All edges equal weight (or unweighted) → BFS
Edges have different weights → Dijkstra

Keywords: "minimum cost", "shortest path", "minimum steps",
"minimum distance", "time to reach"

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUB-TYPE A — BFS (Unweighted / Equal weights)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

BFS visits nodes in order of distance from the source.
First time you reach a node = shortest path to it.

vector<int> dist(n, -1);
queue<int> q;
q.push(start);
dist[start] = 0;

while (!q.empty()) {
    int node = q.front(); q.pop();
    for (int nb : adj[node]) {
        if (dist[nb] == -1) {
            dist[nb] = dist[node] + 1;
            q.push(nb);
        }
    }
}
EXAMPLE — 01 Matrix
https://leetcode.com/problems/01-matrix

Multi-source BFS: push ALL 0-cells into the queue at level 0.
Spread outward. First time a 1-cell is reached = its distance.

EXAMPLE — Rotting Oranges
https://leetcode.com/problems/rotting-oranges

Multi-source BFS from all rotten oranges simultaneously.
Answer = BFS levels elapsed when last fresh orange rots.
Count fresh oranges. If any remain after BFS → return -1.

EXAMPLE — Word Ladder
https://leetcode.com/problems/word-ladder

BFS where each edge = words differing by exactly one letter.
For each word, try replacing every character with a-z.
Answer = level at which target word is reached.

─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SUB-TYPE B — DIJKSTRA (Weighted, non-negative edges)
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Like BFS but uses a min-heap (priority queue) instead of a queue.
Always expand the node with the smallest known distance first.

vector<int> dist(n, INT_MAX);
priority_queue<pair<int,int>, vector<pair<int,int>>, greater<>> pq;
// min-heap on {distance, node}

dist[src] = 0;
pq.push({0, src});

while (!pq.empty()) {
    auto [d, u] = pq.top(); pq.pop();
    if (d > dist[u]) continue;   // stale entry — skip

    for (auto [v, w] : adj[u]) {
        if (dist[u] + w < dist[v]) {
            dist[v] = dist[u] + w;
            pq.push({dist[v], v});
        }
    }
}
EXAMPLE — Network Delay Time
https://leetcode.com/problems/network-delay-time

Single-source Dijkstra from node k.
Answer = max(dist). If any node is unreachable → return -1.

EXAMPLE — Path With Minimum Effort
https://leetcode.com/problems/path-with-minimum-effort

Dijkstra on a grid. Edge weight = |height difference|.
dist[r][c] = minimum "effort" (max edge on path) to reach (r,c).
Relax: effort = max(current effort, |grid[nr][nc] - grid[r][c]|).

EXAMPLE — Cheapest Flights Within K Stops
https://leetcode.com/problems/cheapest-flights-within-k-stops

Dijkstra with state = {cost, node, stops_used}.
Only relax edges if stops_used < k.

