TYPE 3 — TOPOLOGICAL SORT (DAG + Dependencies)
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

When to use:
→ Directed graph with dependencies
→ Find a valid ordering of tasks
→ Detect cycle in a directed graph

Keywords: "prerequisites", "course schedule", "task dependencies",
"build order", "finish all tasks"

Key idea: process nodes with 0 incoming edges (in-degree 0) first.
After processing, reduce the in-degree of their neighbors.
If a neighbor's in-degree becomes 0 → add it to the queue.

KAHN'S ALGORITHM (BFS-based topo sort):

vector<int> indegree(n, 0);
for (int u = 0; u < n; u++)
    for (int v : adj[u])
        indegree[v]++;

queue<int> q;
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
    if (indegree[i] == 0) q.push(i);

vector<int> order;
while (!q.empty()) {
    int u = q.front(); q.pop();
    order.push_back(u);
    for (int v : adj[u])
        if (--indegree[v] == 0)
            q.push(v);
}

// order.size() == n → valid DAG (no cycle)
// order.size() <  n → cycle exists
EXAMPLE — Course Schedule
https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule

prerequisites[i] = [a, b] means b must come before a → edge b → a.
Run Kahn's. If order.size() == numCourses → all courses can finish.

EXAMPLE — Course Schedule II
https://leetcode.com/problems/course-schedule-ii

Same setup. Return the order vector (valid course sequence).
If cycle detected → return empty vector.

EXAMPLE — Parallel Courses III (Hard)
https://leetcode.com/problems/parallel-courses-iii

Topological sort + DP on the DAG.
time[v] = max(time[u] for all u → v) + time[v].
Answer = max time across all nodes.

