BIPARTITE CHECK (2-coloring with BFS):

A graph is bipartite if you can color every node with 2 colors
such that no two adjacent nodes share the same color.
Equivalent: no odd-length cycle exists.

vector<int> color(n, -1);

bool check(int start, vector<vector<int>>& adj) {
    queue<int> q;
    q.push(start);
    color[start] = 0;
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int u = q.front(); q.pop();
        for (int v : adj[u]) {
            if (color[v] == -1) {
                color[v] = 1 - color[u];
                q.push(v);
            } else if (color[v] == color[u]) {
                return false;   // same color on both ends = not bipartite
            }
        }
    }
    return true;
}
EXAMPLE — Is Graph Bipartite?
https://leetcode.com/problems/is-graph-bipartite

Run bipartite check on every unvisited node (graph may be disconnected).
If any component fails → return false.

CYCLE DETECTION IN DIRECTED GRAPH (DFS with 3 states):

For directed graphs, a 2-state visited array is not enough.
A "back edge" (visiting a node currently on the stack) = cycle.
You need 3 states:
0 = unvisited
1 = currently on DFS stack (being explored)
2 = fully done (no cycle found through this node)

vector<int> state(n, 0);

bool hasCycle(int u, vector<vector<int>>& adj) {
    state[u] = 1;
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        if (state[v] == 1) return true;    // back edge = cycle
        if (state[v] == 0 && hasCycle(v, adj)) return true;
    }
    state[u] = 2;
    return false;
}
WHY 3 STATES?
With only 2 states, a cross edge (u → v where v is already fully done)
would be incorrectly flagged as a cycle. State 2 means "done, safe".

EXAMPLE — Find Eventual Safe States
https://leetcode.com/problems/find-eventual-safe-states

A safe node = no cycle is reachable from it.
After DFS, all nodes with state == 2 are safe.
Return them sorted.

