COMMON MISTAKES AND HOW TO AVOID THEM
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

MISTAKE 1 — Marking visited when popping instead of pushing
If you mark when popping, the same node gets added multiple times.
Always mark visited BEFORE pushing into the queue.

MISTAKE 2 — Using BFS for weighted shortest path
BFS only gives the shortest path when all edges have equal weight.
If weights differ → use Dijkstra.

MISTAKE 3 — Using 2-state visited for directed cycle detection
For undirected graphs, 2 states (visited/not) are enough.
For directed graphs, you need 3 states.
2 states will incorrectly flag cross-edges as cycles.

MISTAKE 4 — Not handling disconnected graphs
Always loop over ALL nodes, not just node 0.
Many graph problems have multiple disconnected components.

MISTAKE 5 — Not skipping stale entries in Dijkstra
When you pop {dist, node} from the heap:
if dist > known dist[node] → skip it immediately.
Without this, you reprocess nodes you already found optimal paths for.

